1
00:00:04,659 --> 00:00:10,548
India coastal & near-sea network: 12 real services, from JNPT–Mundra and Chennai–Singapore to Mundra–Rotterdam.

2
00:00:10,548 --> 00:00:17,655
Run the way fleets run today (VLSFO at full schedule speed), it emits about 2.1 Mt CO₂e a year, and 11 of 12 services fail the 2030 CII limit.

3
00:00:22,603 --> 00:00:27,921
Scenario: India 2030. IMO CII, FuelEU Maritime and EU ETS are built in, and every assumption is editable.

4
00:00:27,921 --> 00:00:35,601
QMOEA-H, our quantum-inspired optimiser, decides vessel mix, capacity, speed, fuel and shore power for all 12 routes at once.

5
00:00:35,601 --> 00:00:41,601
Each dot is a complete fleet plan. The front of best trade-offs streams in live, far below today's plans (grey markers).

6
00:00:41,601 --> 00:00:48,777
Done: 116 Pareto-optimal plans out of 6,000 evaluated. The recommended plan sits at the knee of the curve.

7
00:00:48,777 --> 00:00:55,664
Recommended plan: 60 % less well-to-wake GHG, 52 % less fuel and 23 % lower cost than today, with every constraint met.

8
00:00:55,664 --> 00:01:03,484
It explains itself in plain words: which routes switch to LNG or ammonia, where to slow-steam, and where to plug into shore power.

9
00:01:03,484 --> 00:01:10,515
Fleet allocation on the map, and the emission profile of every service.

10
00:01:10,515 --> 00:01:17,552
Per service: vessel class and capacity, number of ships, speed, fuel, shore power and the resulting CII rating.

11
00:01:17,552 --> 00:01:26,497
One click builds a decision report for managers: the plan, compliance, robustness and abatement costs.

12
00:01:31,582 --> 00:01:37,795
For the chosen plan: the CII rating of every service, the FuelEU pool against its tightening limit, and EU ETS exposure.

13
00:01:42,819 --> 00:01:48,769
Q-PHYS predicts fuel from speed, load, weather and vessel type: a physics model plus a quantum-inspired tensor network.

14
00:01:48,769 --> 00:01:56,121
Speed up and fuel rises steeply. The model is certified never to predict less fuel at a higher speed.

15
00:01:56,121 --> 00:02:03,874
Rougher seas add fuel, and the 90 % confidence band scales with the prediction.

16
00:02:03,874 --> 00:02:09,760
Switch the fuel system to e-ammonia: fuel mass and well-to-wake emissions update instantly.

17
00:02:09,760 --> 00:02:15,910
Inside the tensor network: entanglement entropy shows which inputs the model couples, a view borrowed from quantum physics.

18
00:02:20,922 --> 00:02:25,972
2025 → 2050: every milestone year is optimised under that year's rules, prices and fuel availability.

19
00:02:25,972 --> 00:02:33,174
The fuel mix shifts decade by decade as the targets tighten, while emissions keep falling.

20
00:02:33,174 --> 00:02:39,311
Marginal abatement cost curve: which measures save money, and which cost dollars per tonne of CO₂e avoided.

21
00:02:39,311 --> 00:02:45,617
An exact MILP solves the discretised problem to true optimality in about a second: the yardstick for every heuristic.

22
00:02:45,617 --> 00:02:51,767
The same fleet problem as a QUBO, solved by our own simulated quantum annealer and exportable unchanged to D-Wave hardware.

23
00:02:51,767 --> 00:02:56,067
Annealed plan: 1.52% from the exact optimum on the same objective, and fully feasible.

24
00:03:02,027 --> 00:03:10,125
Prediction: Q-PHYS reaches 3.3 % error on known ships, ahead of LightGBM, random forest and neural networks on the same data.

25
00:03:10,125 --> 00:03:16,891
Optimisation: QMOEA-H ranks first against NSGA-II, NSGA-III and MOPSO, and lands within 1–3 % of the exact optimum.

26
00:03:16,891 --> 00:03:22,491
We also show where classical methods still do better, such as MOPSO on very large, loosely constrained networks.

27
00:03:27,239 --> 00:03:37,289
What-if: close the Red Sea. The Europe service reroutes around the Cape of Good Hope (6,348 → 11,027 nm), ready to re-optimise.
